-- Shooter — assertions about a magazine, a line of aliens, and a sprite budget.
--
-- Every case opens with `press a`, because every game opens on its title
-- screen. That is one line of ceremony in exchange for the title screen
-- being part of what the suite checks rather than something it routes
-- around: a game that never reached `play` would fail every case below.

test the alien line advances
press a
play 0.5 seconds
expect a1.x > 4vw - 1

test the alien line turns at the wall instead of leaving it
press a
play 15 seconds
expect a9.right <= screenwidth
expect a1.x >= 0

test the magazine starts parked and inert
press a
expect s1.visible = 0
expect s2.visible = 0
expect s3.visible = 0

test one press launches one shot, upward
press a
press a
play 10 ticks
expect s1.visible = 1
expect s1.y < screenheight - 3
expect s2.visible = 0
expect s3.visible = 0

-- The point of the magazine: three in the air at once, each launched by its own
-- press, each at the height its press put it.
test three presses put three shots in the air
press a
press a
play 8 ticks
press a
play 8 ticks
press a
play 4 ticks
expect s1.visible = 1
expect s2.visible = 1
expect s3.visible = 1
expect s1.y < s2.y
expect s2.y < s3.y

-- A fourth press with nothing in the rack must do nothing at all. The failure it
-- guards against is not "no shot appears" but "an airborne shot is teleported
-- back to the ship", which looks like firing and plays like cheating.
test a fourth press waits for a shot to come back
press a
press a
play 8 ticks
press a
play 8 ticks
press a
play 8 ticks
press a
play 4 ticks
expect s1.y < s2.y
expect s2.y < s3.y

test a shot parks itself again at the top
press a
press a
play 5 seconds
expect s1.visible = 0

test the ship runs to each edge and stops
press a
hold left for 5 seconds
expect player.x = 0
expect player.xdirection = 0
